<!--
  Central Package Management. One version per package for the whole solution.

  Without this, every project pins its own version and they drift: a solution with
  thirty test projects ends up with three MSTest versions and a coverage report that
  silently covers less than it claims. Add PackageVersion here; projects reference
  the package with no Version attribute.

  Aspire package versions MUST match the Aspire.AppHost.Sdk version in global.json,
  and the installed Aspire CLI must match both. A mismatch fails at AppHost startup
  with an error naming the wrong side of it.
-->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Scaffold output. Every project template scaffold.json invokes emits package
    references with an inline Version attribute; central package management rejects
    that outright (NU1008), so the manifest strips the attributes and these entries
    are what is left to decide the version. A package a generator emits and this
    file does not pin fails restore, which is the only thing keeping this group
    honest as the template pack moves - there is no list to read it off.

    Pinned at what the templates shipped with the SDK emitted, except where a
    later version is forced. Two are:

    Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience is at 10.8.0 rather than the template's
    10.6.0 because Aspire.Hosting.Testing 13.5.3 depends on it at >= 10.8.0, and a
    central pin below a transitive floor is a downgrade rather than a choice:

      error NU1109: Detected package downgrade: Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience
      from 10.8.0 to centrally defined 10.7.0.

    Microsoft.Extensions.ServiceDiscovery stays at 10.7.0 despite sharing that
    release band. Nothing in the graph depends on it - the service defaults project
    is the only thing that references it, and it does so directly - so there is no
    floor to clear and no reason to move it. Read the restore error rather than
    assuming the two travel together.

    Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi is at 10.0.12 rather than the template's 10.0.9 to
    clear a vulnerability; see the note above it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Scaffold output">
    <!--
      A version bump that exists only to close a vulnerability, in a package the
      web API template emits.

      10.0.9 - what the template writes - depends on Microsoft.OpenApi >= 2.0.0,
      and 2.0.0 is what resolves. That version is vulnerable, NuGetAudit reports
      it, and TreatWarningsAsErrors turns the report into a failed build:

        error NU1903: Warning As Error: Package 'Microsoft.OpenApi' 2.0.0 has a
        known high severity vulnerability

      GHSA-v5pm-xwqc-g5wc / CVE-2026-49451: a circular schema reference in an
      OpenAPI document recurses without bound, overflows the stack and takes the
      process with it. Affects Microsoft.OpenApi 2.0.0-preview.11 through 2.7.4.

      10.0.12 depends on Microsoft.OpenApi [2.12.0, 3.0.0), so the vulnerable
      version can no longer resolve and the upper bound keeps the 3.x line - which
      carries the same defect until 3.5.4 - out of reach as well.

      10.0.12 is the version the advisory was CLEARED at, not the lowest version
      that clears it. Nobody established which release first drops the vulnerable
      dependency, so treat this as a known-good floor rather than a measured one,
      and re-check the advisory rather than the changelog when moving it.

      Do NOT silence this with NoWarn instead: the audit level here is a deliberate
      step up from the SDK default, and a scaffold that suppresses a high severity
      advisory does it in every repository it creates.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" Version="10.0.12" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience" Version="10.8.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.ServiceDiscovery" Version="10.7.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Exporter.OpenTelemetryProtocol" Version="1.15.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Extensions.Hosting" Version="1.15.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.AspNetCore" Version="1.15.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.Http" Version="1.15.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.Runtime" Version="1.15.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Testing">
    <PackageVersion Include="MSTest" Version="4.2.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Aspire.Hosting.Testing" Version="13.5.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Aspire.Hosting is pinned even though no emitted csproj names it: the
    Aspire.AppHost.Sdk brings it into the AppHost project, and a reference that
    arrives without being typed out is still a reference this file answers for
    once transitive pinning is on. It tracks the SDK version in global.json,
    which the scaffold rewrites the AppHost's inline SDK attribute to match -
    the template emits whichever version happens to be installed.

    Aspire.Hosting.AppHost is deliberately NOT pinned here, and the difference is
    worth knowing before reaching for it. The SDK declares that one as an
    IMPLICIT package reference, and central package management forbids pinning an
    implicit reference outright:

      error NU1009: The following PackageReference items are implicitly defined
      and cannot define a PackageVersion item: Aspire.Hosting.AppHost.

    An implicit reference already carries its version from the SDK that defined
    it, so there is nothing here to decide and adding a line only breaks restore.
    "Injected but explicit" and "implicit" look the same from the csproj, which is
    why this is written down rather than left to be rediscovered.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Aspire">
    <PackageVersion Include="Aspire.Hosting" Version="13.5.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Aspire.Hosting.SqlServer" Version="13.5.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Aspire.Hosting.Redis" Version="13.5.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Aspire.Hosting.Azure.Storage" Version="13.5.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    EF Core. Design is a build-time-only dependency (dotnet ef), so reference it with
    PrivateAssets="all" in the project that owns migrations - otherwise it flows into
    consumers and ships tooling to production.

    Migrations are generated code: they are already excluded from coverage in
    coverage.runsettings and exempted from style rules in the .editorconfig. Both
    exemptions are deliberate. A coverage number that counts migrations is a number
    that lies, and a generated file that fails a style gate stops the build for a
    reason nobody can act on.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Data">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Tools" Version="10.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Web. Blazor and ASP.NET Core themselves come from the shared framework, not from
    packages - these are the pieces that do not: component testing, and the auth
    handlers a deployed Web App or Function App needs.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Web">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Server" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="bunit" Version="1.41.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="ML">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.ML" Version="4.0.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.ML.LightGbm" Version="4.0.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.ML.AutoML" Version="0.23.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.Analysis" Version="0.23.2" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
